  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Published in 2016 by Peter Lang Verlag as part of the established Philosophie et Politique series, <em>Forme(s) et modes d'\u00eatre / Form(s) and Modes of Being</em> by Olivier Malherbe and S\u00e9bastien Richard has generated early scholarly uptake. The work has been cited twice as of 2025, with citations appearing in both journal articles and edited volumes within analytic philosophy and philosophy of language, indicating traction within specialized academic circles.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This bilingual philosophical work addresses questions of form and modes of being at the intersection of physics and philosophy. Its placement within the Philosophie et Politique series\u2014a Peter Lang imprint that has published over 30 volumes since at least 2012\u2014situates it within a sustained community of European philosophical inquiry. The series encompasses works on ethics, democracy, epistemology, and related foundational topics, suggesting that Malherbe and Richard's contribution participates in a broader institutional conversation about metaphysics and the nature of existence.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">Citation evidence reveals engagement from scholars working on phenomenology and analytic philosophy. A 2025 article by Daniel Neumann at the University of Graz, published in the <em>International Journal of Philosophical Studies</em>, cites the book in discussion of Ingarden's theory of ideas. Earlier, in 2020, co-author Olivier Malherbe himself contributed a chapter on meaning in Roman Ingarden's philosophy of language to a volume on the history of analytic philosophy, also citing this work. This pattern\u2014citations appearing both from external scholars and from one of the authors' subsequent work\u2014suggests the book has established a conceptual foothold in discussions of phenomenology and formal ontology.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">The geographic distribution of citing works (Austria and Belgium) and their institutional bases (University of Graz and Universit\u00e9 Libre de Bruxelles) reflect Central and Western European academic networks. The citing works span both journal articles and book chapters, indicating the book's relevance across different publication formats within philosophy. The concepts associated with the citing works\u2014intuition, epistemology, meaning, and axiology\u2014align with the core themes signaled in the original work, suggesting targeted rather than incidental adoption.</p>\n\n<p class=\"story-text\">With two citations on record, this work represents an early-stage scholarly trajectory. No download metrics or public discourse events are captured in available records, limiting visibility of broader reader reception. Library holdings through WorldCat and repository records via OpenAIRE document its archival accessibility, but systematic adoption in teaching or widespread community engagement cannot yet be confirmed from the available evidence.</p>",
